; 19-let.kurt

; `let` introduces one or more fresh, completely arbitrary constants.
; Closing the block (here: by dedenting) triggers "forall-intro": whatever
; you proved about the fresh constant, inside the block, now holds for *all*
; objects, via a `forall`.  The quantifiers come with `logic.kurt`, so a file
; with `let` needs `load logic` (the core has no quantifiers).  A fact
; `forall $x A $x` is stored as the schema `A $x`, so it is instantiated
; directly; the rule "forall-elim" is needed for a `forall` inside a bigger
; formula, e.g.
; in `(∀ $x A $x) implies B`:

; `arity` (below) itself isn't taught until lesson 31 -- used here a little early since a
; demonstration of `forall`/`let` needs *some* predicate that actually takes an argument
; (`arity A 1` just means "A takes one").
load logic
bool A
const A
arity A 1

use forall $x A $x   ; "for every x, A holds of x"

show forall x A x
proof
    let x            ; `x` is a brand-new constant, could be anything
        A x           ; the axiom above, instantiated at `x`
qed                    ; since `x` was arbitrary, this generalises back to `∀ x A x`

; `let` can also attach a condition to the new constant, e.g. `let x > 0` --
; once you have a theory (like `numbers.kurt`) with `>` in it.  Closing the block
; then gives a quantifier with a condition, `forall x > 0 ...`; the rules of
; `logic.kurt` say what it means, e.g. "forall-cond-def" turns it into
; `forall x (x > 0 implies ...)`, which is what `let x` followed by `assume x > 0` gives.
